3. Gate G2 — R1 Mechanics and Geotechnics

3.1 Ground pressure versus bearing capacity

The target ground pressure of 15 kPa against a lowest cited bearing strength of 2.2 kPa gives a ratio of 6.8×. This is not a margin; it is a potential sinkage condition.

Measured cohesion across landed sites:

Site Cohesion c Friction angle φ Note
Phoenix 0.2–1.2 kPa 29–47° [S]
Pathfinder 0.12–0.36 kPa 21–32° [S]
MER Spirit 0.5–15 kPa 20–25° [S]
Zhurong, Utopia Planitia (25.07° N) 1.5–6 kPa 21°–34° Equivalent stiffness 1,390–5,872 kPa/m^N; not our 40° N site [S]

Cohesion spans roughly 0.2 kPa to 15 kPa — nearly two orders of magnitude. A single bearing criterion is invalid. The risk is never the mean of the distribution; it is the layer that was not tested. Site-specific penetrometry is mandatory before any pressure target is fixed [V].

3.2 Cutting force against the closure criterion

The closure criterion for R1 is:

F_{\text{cut}} \;\le\; 0.7 \times F_{\text{traction, net}}

With R1 at 2,800 kg, μ = 0.7, and Mars gravity 3.71 m/s²:

F_{\text{traction, gross}} = 0.7 \times 2{,}800 \times 3.71 = 7.27\ \text{kN}

A simplified McKyes estimate for scarified soil (c ≈ 1.5 kPa, φ = 30°, N_γ ≈ 25, N_c ≈ 40, bucket 0.5 m × 0.15 m) gives a base cutting force near 6.1 kN, rising to 7.3–9.1 kN with three-dimensional and dynamic amplification.

0.7 \times 7.27 = \mathbf{5.09\ kN\ allowable}

The amplified range of 7.3–9.1 kN is 1.4–1.8× the allowable. This is not a marginal overrun of gross traction; it is a substantial overrun of the design criterion, before rolling resistance, slope, turning losses, or slip are subtracted.

Bucket narrowing is a fallback, not the plan. A 0.4 m bucket gives 5.8–7.3 kN, still 1.1–1.4× the allowable, and it concedes productivity to preserve a zero-margin force balance.

3.3 Percussive excavation — attacking the soil side of the equation

The primary answer is to reduce the cutting force rather than increase the traction. Documented reductions:

Reference Force reduction Conditions
VIPER / NASA KSC percussive bucket up to 50% 30-inch pivoting bucket, laboratory simulant
Honeybee Robotics percussive scoop ×6 (≈83%) Compacted JSC-1A, ~250 N insertion force

R1 requirement: a percussive or vibratory cutting edge, with ≥40% force reduction demonstrated on a characterized Mars simulant before R1 is declared feasible.

At the 9.1 kN high end, a 40% reduction yields 5.5 kN — still marginally above the 5.09 kN allowable. Closing with reserve therefore requires the percussive edge plus one of: bucket width reduction to ~0.45 m, a traction increase, or a slope/compaction reserve. This is a test target, not a result.

3.4 The mass–traction coupling may be avoidable

R1 buys 2,800 kg for traction. That mass then drives ground pressure (§3.1) and delivered mass (§2.1). This coupling is not physically necessary. NASA's RASSOR uses counter-rotating bucket drums that provide near-zero horizontal and minimal vertical net reaction force, so excavation does not rely on vehicle weight or traction [S].

Required trade study, not performed: drum excavator versus blade-plus-traction with a percussive edge. The figure of merit is net drawbar pull (gross thrust minus motion resistance), not a gross friction coefficient. A lightweight drum platform would relieve ground pressure, delivered mass, and cutting force simultaneously.

3.5 Ground ice at 40° N — the soil model may be wrong in kind

Ground ice is stable poleward of roughly 40° latitude. At 40–55° N, models place the ice table at 25–150 cm depth in some terrains (41–255 cm in others), with strong longitudinal variation. SWIM-class orbital mapping provides ice-consistency indices over the 0–1 m, 1–5 m, and >5 m depth ranges at ~3 km per pixel.

If ice-cemented regolith lies within excavation depth, cohesive strength is orders of magnitude above the dry-soil inputs of §3.2, and the cutting-force range computed there is invalidated in kind, not merely in parameter value.

G2 requirements: an orbital ice-consistency assessment for the candidate site (G7), an ice-cemented simulant in the cutting and traction matrix, a duricrust simulant at 0.3–0.7 MPa tip resistance, and a loose-sand rebound case for penetrometer qualification.

3.6 Penetrometry — the InSight lesson

The InSight HP³ instrument was a percussive penetrometer carrying a hammer, a suppressor mass, and spring recoil absorption. It reached 40 cm against a 3–5 m target after two years of recovery attempts. Two failure regimes are directly relevant:

1. Too cohesive. A near-surface duricrust with 0.3–0.7 MPa penetration resistance arrested the mole; a deeper layer measured 4.9 ± 0.4 MPa. Mars-wide duricrust cohesion inferred from the mission was ~5.8 kPa — inside the Zhurong range quoted in §3.1. The nominal soil class produces this layer.
2. Too cohesionless. In loose sand without sufficient hull friction, percussion converts to precession and backward ejection instead of penetration.

A percussive-only penetrometer therefore fails in both tails of the soil distribution. The instrument class selected here is rotary-percussive, with flight heritage on Curiosity: rotation coupled to a voice-coil percussion mechanism, and rotary-percussion that succeeded even in improvised "feed-extended drilling" mode, where the robotic arm supplied the downforce.

This also solves the reaction-mass problem. R4 at 280 kg weighs ~1 kN on Mars. An arm-preloaded rotary-percussive cone requires on the order of 100–300 N of downforce. R4 is its own reaction mass, exactly as Curiosity's arm is. A static penetrometer is not impossible in 0.38 g; it is unanchored. Anchoring to the rover, plus rotation to defeat duricrust, plus percussion to assist in loose sand, is the configuration specified.

