4.3 Fission against load — the decisive arithmetic

Baseline architecture: Kilopower-class, eight 1,250 We Stirling converters, 10 kWe nominal.

10\ \text{kWe} \times 24\ \text{h} = 240\ \text{kWh/sol}

8.75\ \text{kWe (one converter failed)} \times 24 = 210\ \text{kWh/sol}

210 kWh/sol is 2.3× the 90 kWh/sol reference and 1.6× the 135 kWh/sol high bound. The fission source alone covers the load with a converter failed. If a four-converter architecture is assumed instead, one failure leaves 7.5 kWe and 180 kWh/sol — still 2× the reference.

Continuous electrical production does not demonstrate thermal survival. The transient model does not exist.

4.4 Stirling failure model must be architecture-specific

Architecture Converters Loss of one Dominant failure driver
Kilopower-class 10 kWe 8 × 1.25 kWe −12.5% → 8.75 kWe Controller card
FSP-class 40 kWe 4 × 12 kWe −25% → 30 kWe Converter and controller

The controller card is a first-order reliability term, not a rounding error. A redundant controller raises string reliability from roughly 90% to roughly 96% [S]. Direct-coupled architectures can propagate a power-conversion fault into core thermal management, which is why intermediate heat exchangers are studied.

Requirements: the survival case must state which architecture is assumed, must model controller-card failure rather than converters alone, and must state whether an intermediate heat exchanger is included [V].

4.5 Battery — three temperature limits, not one

Below 0 °C, charging a lithium-ion cell causes lithium plating — permanent capacity loss and a safety hazard. Charging therefore has a harder floor than discharging. MER heritage operated batteries within roughly −20 to +40 °C.

Limit Value Basis
Storage survival ≥ −40 °C Heater duty carried in §4.2
Discharge operation ≥ −20 °C MER heritage
Charging ≥ 0 °C; pre-heat required below Lithium-plating constraint
Nominal capacity ≥ 111 kWh 80 kWh usable, DoD 0.8, η 0.9
Mass at 120–150 Wh/kg 740–930 kg [C]

Derivation of capacity:

C_{\text{nom}} = \frac{80}{0.8 \times 0.9} = 111\ \text{kWh}

Power requirements: continuous discharge ≥ 15 kW, peak discharge ≥ 37 kW for 10 minutes, charge ≥ 10 kW.

Autonomy rule: no charging when cell temperature is below 0 °C without pre-heat. Heater energy per mode is cross-referenced to §4.2.

4.6 Solar — not in the baseline

PV is excluded because (a) the fission source covers the load by more than 2× even at reduced converter capacity, (b) dust deposition runs 0.2% per sol typical, 0.05–2% per sol range, giving roughly 18% loss over 90 sols and 45% over 300 sols without cleaning [S], and (c) no stated function justifies 800 kg of array.

PV may return only with an explicit use case, duty cycle, and cleaning model. If retained, it must be sized at ≤5% of clear-sky production during storm — Opportunity's stored energy fell from 645 Wh to 22 Wh, about 3%, at optical depth 10.8 [S].

4.7 Seasonal, not annual

A "3 equivalent sun-hours" figure is a spring/summer value at 40° N. Winter falls to 1.5–2 h, and storms suppress production further. Seasonal and storm profiles (τ = 0.5 through 5+) are required before any array is sized [V].

Status: OPEN. Survival with one converter failed, battery within its three limits, must be shown by transient model and test. Neither exists.

