5. Gate G4 — Storm Survival

5.1 Design case: 90 sols, 2018 profile

The 2018 global dust storm is the modern benchmark:

· Growth from late May, peak in early July, decay through October.
· Optical depth exceeded 10.7, the highest ever measured.
· Extinction decay timescale 43 sols — weeks of near-darkness after the peak.
· Global storms are documented to persist for weeks to months.

A 30-sol test certifies survival of an average regional event, not the global case. The design case is 90 sols with the 2018 optical-depth profile as input.

One mitigating effect, free: storms suppress the diurnal temperature swing — from nearly 100 K down to about 40 K at Gale — so survival heating load drops during the worst case. Only a transient model captures this, which is G3.

5.2 The test required

A 90-sol, zero-solar, one-converter-failed, battery-heating-active test in a relevant environment, with all rover work suspended and communications reduced to alert-only.

Pass criteria: battery above −40 °C in storage and above −20 °C in discharge; electronics within qualification limits; no irreversible degradation; safe state maintained throughout.

Status: OPEN. No thermal-vacuum test has been performed.

