11. Design doctrine

Principle Statement
Local autonomy with an independent safety layer Physical limits cannot wait for Earth light-time or relay availability
Separation of geometric and geotechnical verdicts A level surface is not a load-bearing surface; cohesion spans two orders of magnitude
Temporal energy accounting Mode profiles, not global factors; peak, average, and energy are distinct quantities
Anchor the instrument to the vehicle Reaction force comes from the rover's own weight, not from an unanchored mass
Attack the soil side before the machine side Force reduction through percussion precedes mass increase for traction
Plan against demonstrated capability Mission architecture is sized against what has flown, not against what is claimed
Calculation defines the test A conditional calculation cannot close a gate that requires a measurement
Fail on paper first Every gate is expected to fail at the calculation stage before it fails in hardware

