APPENDIX D — Penetrometry and Excavation (G2, G6)

D.1 HP³ — both failure modes, quantified

The InSight HP³ mole was a percussive penetrometer with a hammer, suppressor mass, and recoil absorption. It reached 40 cm against a 3–5 m target. The penetration record yields:

Layer Penetration resistance Source
Duricrust 0.3–0.7 MPa HP³ soil-properties paper 
Deeper layer (>30 cm) 4.9 ± 0.4 MPa idem 

The diagnosed root cause is a lack of friction in an unexpectedly thick cohesive duricrust . Mars-wide duricrust cohesion inferred from the mission is ~5.8 kPa, inside the Zhurong range — the nominal soil class produces this layer.

Two failure regimes:

1. Too cohesive: duricrust at 0.3–0.7 MPa arrests the mole.
2. Too cohesionless: in loose sand without hull friction, percussion converts to precession and backward ejection.

A percussive-only penetrometer therefore fails in both tails of the soil distribution.

D.2 Rotary-percussive Curiosity — flight heritage

Curiosity’s drill couples rotation with a voice-coil percussion mechanism: “percussive energy is provided by a voice-coil mechanism that uses a magnetic field to oscillate a free mass, which acts as a hammer and transfers energy to the drill bit” . Six voice-coil energy levels are available .

The arm preloads the drill against the target; a typical preload is ~300 N . Rotary-percussion succeeded even in improvised “feed-extended drilling” mode, where the arm supplied the downforce .

Application to R4: R4 at 280 kg weighs ~1,039 N on Mars (280 × 3.71). An arm-preloaded rotary-percussive cone needs 100–300 N. R4 is its own reaction mass, exactly as Curiosity’s arm is.

D.3 Percussive force reduction — the robust figures

Reference Reduction Conditions Source
KSC large-scale bucket (Mueller et al.) 19.24% average All parameter permutations, laboratory simulant 
KSC 30-inch pivoting bucket up to 50–51% Percussion tangential to motion 
Honeybee percussive scoop (JSC-1A) 15× reduction in downforce for penetration Compacted lunar simulant 

Correction: the “15×” figure is a downforce reduction for penetration, not a bucket cutting-force reduction. The robust bucket-cutting figures are 19.24% average and 50% maximum. The project target of ≥40% is a test target, not a result.

D.4 RASSOR — decoupling mass from traction

RASSOR is a 66 kg micro-excavator with two counter-rotating bucket drums:

“RASSOR uses counterrotating bucket drums on opposing arms to provide near-zero horizontal and minimal vertical net reaction force so that excavation is not reliant on the traction or weight of the mobility system” .

“These drums are designed to dig and store regolith simultaneously, as well as cancel the reaction forces caused by digging by rotating the bucket drums in opposing directions” .

Implication: R1’s “mass bought for traction” coupling is not physically necessary. A drum excavator can be lightweight, relieving ground pressure (§A.3) and delivered mass simultaneously.

