E.3 Battery — three temperature limits

JPL qualified low-temperature Li-ion battery technology for Mars surface missions at −20 to +40 °C . A development programme reports cells qualified to −40 to +45 °C for storage, with operational limits narrower .

Charging below 0 °C causes lithium plating — permanent capacity loss and a safety hazard. Plating is facilitated by high charge voltages, high currents, and poor anode kinetics . At −30 °C with C/5 rates, plating can be suppressed but not eliminated; at −50 °C, plating reaches ~11% of total capacity .

Limit Value Basis
