APPENDIX G — Ground Ice and Programmatics (G2, G7)

G.1 Ground ice at 40° N — first-order risk

Ice emplaced at 40–55° N during a past high-obliquity period (4.18 Myr ago) would today reside at depths of 25–150 cm (41–255 cm) depending on regolith and ice properties .

SWIM thermal analysis identifies layering consistent with shallow ice between 40° N and 60° N . The SWIM project targets the 40–52° N band for focused multi-dataset mapping . Shallow ground ice is described as widespread at depths <1 m at high latitudes, typically poleward of ~40° N .

Implication: 40° N is the transition latitude. Ice-cemented regolith is a first-order possibility within excavation depth. The dry-soil model of §A.4 is invalid in kind, not merely in parameter value.

G.2 NSPM-20 — the three tiers

NSPM-20 defines three launch-authorisation tiers for space nuclear systems . The criteria:

· Tier III applies to any space nuclear system using nuclear fuel other than low-enriched uranium — including HEU .
· Tier III also applies where the probability of a member of the public receiving a dose above 25 mrem exceeds 1 in 100, or 5 rem exceeds 1 in 1,000,000 .
· “The President’s authorization shall be required for Federal Government launches in Tier III” .

Implication: an HEU-fuelled reactor falls automatically in Tier III. This is a programmatic gate that can invalidate the schedule before any engineering gate is tested.

